There are some separate issues here.

1.  Add new distributions, like

    real wrappedCauchy_lpdf(real phi | reals rho, reals mu)

2.  Add new general primitive functions

    vector change_bearing(vec x, vec y);

3.  Add some HMM-specific densities

    real hmm(vector[] y | vector[] theta, vector[] phi, function Foo);

The arguments must be arrays of vectors making up
the observation sequence y, transition simplexes theta,
and state-specific parameters phi, where Foo is going to
have to be a function that generates the observation densities
for state k using parameters phi[k].

4.  Write a high-level R package like moveHMM that wraps up whole models
some options.  For that, you want to look at RStanArm and the R package
skeleton to which Ben was referring.  If you can prespecify a finite
number of Stan models (perhaps conditional) following RStanArm, it
makes the derived package much simpler to install than RStan itself.

- Bob

